    "created_at": "2022-04-12T15:09:40Z",
    "updated_at": "2022-04-12T15:15:13Z",
    "body": "It is possible to colour the *whole* line, at least in zsh. For my purposes I want a \"black\" background for the prompt, so I'll use `\\e[40m` in my example\r\n```\r\nPS1=\"%{$bg[black]%} $ \"\r\npreexec () { echo '\\e[0m' }\r\n```\r\ndoes the trick.\r\n\r\nHowever, starship would reset the colour. This seems to be a hard problem to solve, since starship depends on `ansi_term` for colouring, which provides an abstraction that one colours *strings* rather than simply changing colours. At the end of every string `ansi_term` resets the colour with '\\e[0m'. If we could change the `RESET` value to `\\e[0m\\e[40m`, that would do it for me. [Relevant `ansi_term` code](https://docs.rs/crate/ansi_term/latest/source/src/ansi.rs)\r\n\r\nBut I'm not sure the elegant way to do this at a higher level. Maybe making a subclass that has a different `Suffix` function? (I'm not much of a rust programmer)",
